Q: Azure AD B2C Output claims A: <p><code>email</code> is listed as an <code>InputClaim</code>. If an input claim already has a value when the step runs, B2C skips rendering it. So check what's populating it upstream. A claims transformation, an earlier orchestration step, or a <code>login_hint</code> on the request will all seed <code>email</code> silently.</p>
<p>Grab the effective policy and compare:</p>
<pre><code>GET https://graph.microsoft.com/beta/trustFramework/policies/B2C_1A_YourPolicy/$value
</code></pre>
<p>Easy way for a CI/CD refactor to change inheritance. either way, stop relying on <code>OutputClaims</code> to render fields:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;DisplayClaims&gt;
  &lt;DisplayClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId=&quot;email&quot; Required=&quot;true&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/DisplayClaims&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How can I convert Intersystems Cache Global Objects to SQL or a more readable format? A: <p>Chances are you dont need to export anything at all of those globals sit behind a persistent class, they are already exposed as SQL tables. Connect via ODBC/JDBC and run normal SQL queries. Check: Management Portal - Systems Explorer - SQL. If the table's there, you're done.</p>
<p>Also .gof is a format backing up/moving globals between instances, not for human- or SQL-readable output. Wrong tool for this. If the global isn't mapped to any class you've got two options:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If the subscript structure is regular, define a class with a global mapping over the existing global, then it becomes queryable via SQL without moving the data.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If not, write ObjectScript routine that walks the global with <code>$Order/$Data</code> and dumps it to CSV</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>  Set file = ##class(%Stream.FileCharacter).%New()
  Set file.Filename = &quot;&quot;C:\export\mydata.csv&quot;&quot;
  Set key1 = &quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;

  For {
      Set key1 = $Order(^MyGlobal(key1))
      Quit:key1 = &quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;
      Set key2 = &quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;

      For {
          Set key2 = $Order(^MyGlobal(key1, key2), 1, value)
          Quit:key 2= &quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;
          Do file.WriteLine(key1 _ &quot;&quot;,&quot;&quot; _ key2 _ &quot;&quot;,&quot;&quot; _ value)
      }
  }

  Do file.%Save()
</code></pre>
